EXT. HIGH DESERT ROADHOUSE, NEW MEXICO TERRITORY - DUSK (1880)

A shuttered adobe at the end of a long road. A tied post with no horse. Tumbleweeds crossing and recrossing a yard of cracked clay. The wind that comes before weather.

Nothing moves. A shutter taps. A tin sign squeals on one hinge.

INT. ROADHOUSE - CONTINUOUS

A room of overturned chairs and cold ashes. A bar stripped of bottles. On the bar, a lantern, and beside the lantern a folded letter propped against a glass.

A boot on the porch. The door creaks. A long shadow falls across the floor.

AMOS WREN (50s, weathered duster, hat the color of old tobacco) stops on the threshold. He studies the room. The chairs. The ashes. The letter.

He takes off his hat. Smooths his hair with one palm. Holds the hat against his chest, as though in church.

AMOS
(under his breath)
Well, Amos. You came all this way.

He crosses to the bar. His spurs ring once, loud, then he stills them with a hand on his thigh. He lifts the letter. Turns it over. Her hand, small and slanted. His name.

He reads without moving his lips. Then, quietly, aloud.

AMOS (V.O.)
(her words, in his voice)
"Amos. If you've come, then I was wrong about you, and I am glad of it. If you have not, then no one is reading this, and I may say anything."

He stops. Lowers the paper an inch. Looks at the door. Looks at the letter again.

AMOS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
"The house is yours. The land was never ours, only lent. Sell the stove. Burn the rest. Do not come looking for the grave; there isn't one. I asked the wind."

His thumb moves over the line. Once. Twice. His jaw works. He sets the hat on the bar, very precisely, brim down.

AMOS
(to the empty room)
Asked the wind.

He folds the letter along its old creases. Slides it inside his coat, over his heart, and pats it flat. Lifts the lantern. The flame leans, steadies, burns clean.

AMOS (CONT'D)
(hoarse)
All right, Lottie.

He turns the lantern low. Walks to the door. Stops with one hand on the frame, and for a long while looks out at the road he came by. The sun is a bruise on the horizon. Wind lifts his coat.

AMOS (CONT'D)
(a short laugh)
Never could win an argument with you.

He sets the lantern on the sill, where its light will show from the road. Steps out. Pulls the door shut behind him, gently, as if not to wake anyone.
